#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748} :activity/id #uuid "58252d2b-905f-529d-99aa-1e8c7d226207" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050785 :topic/id #uuid "66e72e13-4959-5704-8bb3-c0ffea916dd8" :topic/math-academy-id 1384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107031 :tutorial/content "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a2dd1f-90e1-5bcc-a854-1debabaffe1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1338 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5567f868-361e-566c-80a1-f886af15f9ca" :step/math-academy-id 2651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105673 :question/id #uuid "065919d1-7e77-5258-ba32-50b4a2a62942" :question/math-academy-id "e-775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2838ed19-abed-560e-8721-09afa1d696ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106441}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Right-Sided Limit of a Rational Function at a Vertical Asymptote"} :step/id #uuid "3e10f3c1-5b3b-5adb-9fd0-0a1e4d8f3f23" :step/math-academy-id 2652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105674 :question/id #uuid "af0efa71-6027-5026-bd98-f7af0631ccde" :question/math-academy-id "e-776"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b5db1f-0fb2-50af-a851-045cc8cd1d9c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511570}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Left-Sided Limit of a Rational Function at a Vertical Asymptote"} :step/id #uuid "bcefc791-a469-5133-b984-bf5f1294532f" :step/math-academy-id 2654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105675 :question/id #uuid "779e33dc-0411-5cef-a505-df1e5a5c070e" :question/math-academy-id "e-777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a13f946-b2fa-5da3-a09d-6a8086102b94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511581}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "ade971db-c5ab-5cf8-bede-d4db99d2b5f8" :step/math-academy-id 2656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105678 :question/id #uuid "4228f47a-3a9b-54e4-82a6-b72837039d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969d32c6-b343-5e1f-9521-94ff346645b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511462}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Function when Simplification is Required"} :step/id #uuid "c44388fb-c586-558c-b35f-1e1ef27bb6fd" :step/math-academy-id 2660}] :activity/title "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106008}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511641} :activity/id #uuid "4239e5b8-df98-589b-8983-f1548e3897b5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050785 :topic/id #uuid "66e72e13-4959-5704-8bb3-c0ffea916dd8" :topic/math-academy-id 1384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c755ef0-18b5-547d-8180-967d0f618313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510847} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4638dc-f551-5ea6-ad1e-38bbce06b66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510849} {:answer/id #uuid "56c43f1a-e811-5df5-80d4-0515dde8f4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510968} {:answer/id #uuid "694b9ed0-79d0-5201-a364-4e466472a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511005} {:answer/id #uuid "d58b2e03-f857-5968-865a-bc3121726950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511220} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ab316-18be-5962-b3fa-f8deb2262af0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" :question/math-academy-id "q-78040" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nSince $x=1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $0.9$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-4.74$ | $≈-49.75$ | $≈-499.75$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1dbd1e97af1e0fc6a7776fba28f6c4bcb081fa3213291f66aeefc8b3219331e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33eae5f0-498f-582b-b676-c5040fa8abdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510896} {:answer/id #uuid "3521dd0a-297d-5d89-a8e1-a83dd7dd1b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510898} {:answer/id #uuid "3748cfea-f6c8-51ad-a6c6-518385d62fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510907} {:answer/id #uuid "b5afdbe2-4f04-54a7-aa79-406d39105261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511158} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e1e2ae-c02d-5af9-b266-de30c5fe9973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510907} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ceef66-3510-5051-9c39-4e1661e3d26d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107281" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+4)}{(x+4)(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne -4,1,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=1.$ So statement I is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x),$ and  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $1$ | $1.001$ | $1.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-99$ | $-999$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 1$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 1$ from the right. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement II is false and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3de7d0a26f6f171fc83bd885eed9ff9f50bddfc76b2f58e9477241fb874c9b7d.png)"} :step/id #uuid "3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4c8745-9f58-5a6c-9cbc-4855147f632e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510925} {:answer/id #uuid "709ab39f-331d-503b-aa95-5e11144f249b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511017} {:answer/id #uuid "8024b692-ee13-569d-81cc-14158672262e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511044} {:answer/id #uuid "be26a0d0-9356-5266-83e1-5aa7d696bdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511176} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b98644-4bed-5e98-8e64-5be6f13b0782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510925} :answer-field/id #uuid "63236c73-41ba-5765-87b1-d084c15dd995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" :question/math-academy-id "q-107245" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-2),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-2$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-2.01$ | $-2.001$ | $-2$ | $-1.999$ | $-1.99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈40.12$ | $≈400.12$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $≈-399.88$ | $≈-39.88$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-2)$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-2)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is false and II is true.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is also true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25e672e71c4268193c128687ba35eeca1e4bce8f0c355922a3b5df022740159c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1c7a04-1844-590e-89eb-92daf805c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510850} {:answer/id #uuid "29dafe97-f2a8-5f11-9a83-2e51d18852d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510869} {:answer/id #uuid "afd46965-858b-55ad-b7e4-7b4b130bc8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511148} {:answer/id #uuid "dae01dda-cbb0-5a2e-9a59-2709b27b4ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511232} {:answer/id #uuid "f24b746d-d027-5e07-8642-d8dffa243892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511148} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e1baa0-7895-58ed-a291-bc1c098ba28f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" :question/math-academy-id "q-68052" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=1$ and $x=-5.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-5),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-5$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-5.1$ | $-5.01$ | $-5$ | $-4.99$ | $-4.9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-8.36$ | $-83.36$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $83.30$ | $8.30$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-5)$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-5)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and II is false.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-5$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41983c0d07e355a9bd12cf92af158c95f390a5785f4bbc1d08562c240f190623.png)"} :step/id #uuid "98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9836bf91-530a-5113-aa0b-4b54008e48b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511098} {:answer/id #uuid "99621082-2878-5089-ab1f-aeea1c97f48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511100} {:answer/id #uuid "d69edd01-9cb8-5912-94ab-f492c7bb371a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511222} {:answer/id #uuid "dde611b0-cfe1-5a6c-90c9-dcd8bddf724c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511241} {:answer/id #uuid "ff773bb6-564d-50b3-9960-3f4c2adcc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511100} :answer-field/id #uuid "921f7efe-43d0-57f9-9875-d7de999ae3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78042" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+3x+x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{x(x+3)+1(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(x+1)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nSince $x=-1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $-1.1$ | $-1.01$ | $-1.001$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-23.21$ | $≈-203.02$ | $≈-2\\,003.00$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2967a50cc89632da9292fc9192232f757ff3f3bec9304042ef6ec074882565b5.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d4ef3044-b4ec-5d12-92c9-015fe1643574" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511620}}] :activity/title "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510758}]}
